Fine-grained graphite (POCO AF-5) is reported to reduce tool wear in EDM of Hastelloy C-22, while cryogenically treated brass electrodes show the largest reported reductions in electrode wear rate in nanopowder-mixed EDM of Al6061. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}}

### Fine-grained graphite (Hastelloy C-22)
Electrical discharge machining with fine-grained graphite electrodes increases process efficiency and reduces tool wear for Hastelloy C-22 using POCO AF-5 (1 µm) compared to coarser graphite. {{cite:S3#c1}}  
Conversely, coarser graphite (POCO EDM-180, 10 µm) exhibits higher relative tool wear under similar tests. {{cite:S1#c1}}

### Cryogenically treated brass (Al6061, NPMEDM)
Cryogenically treated brass electrodes produced the largest reported reductions in electrode wear rate in the nanopowder-mixed EDM experiments on Al6061, with a 78.41% reduction in EWR for the CT brass electrode versus untreated conditions. {{cite:S7#c1}}  
Non-treated brass and other dielectric/media choices also showed substantial improvements but less than CT brass in that study. {{cite:S7#c1}}

### What these sources do not cover
None of the cited studies directly compare tool wear between graphite (for Hastelloy) and brass (for Al6061) under the same workpiece, EDM mode, or parameter set, so a head-to-head ranking across materials is not available. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S7#c1}}

