Direct answer: Peak current (amperage) is the single most influential parameter on material removal rate (MRR), with pulse-on time / pulse duration as the next most important factor. 

### Peak current (amperage)
Multiple experimental studies found that peak current (amperage) dominates MRR, explaining the largest share of variance in designed experiments. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}} {{cite:S1#c1}}  

### Pulse-on time / pulse duration
Pulse-on time or pulse duration is repeatedly identified as a major driver of MRR alongside current, and is frequently the second-most important factor in Taguchi and factorial studies. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S1#c1}}  

### Electrode material / grain size (secondary influence)
Electrode material and its properties (for example graphite grain size) affect process efficiency and thus MRR, with finer-grained graphite reported to increase process efficiency and reduce tool wear under comparable electrical conditions. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S1#c1}}  

### What these sources do not cover
