Heat treatment transforms as-built SLM maraging steel from a highly refined, as-printed martensitic microstructure with high residual stress into a precipitate-strengthened and (depending on temperature) partially reverted-austenite-containing microstructure, while relieving residual stresses and altering hardness and corrosion behaviour. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Microstructure of the as-printed state
- As-printed SLM maraging steel is a fine-grained martensitic matrix with submicron grain size and high cooling rates, containing little or no equilibrium precipitates. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}}

Precipitation and age hardening after conventional ageing
- Ageing produces massive needle-shaped nanoprecipitates (Ni3X, X = Ti, Al, Mo) inside the martensitic matrix that provide age hardening via Orowan bowing and coherency strain mechanisms, and ageing gives maximum nano-hardness. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

Stress relief and anisotropy change
- Heat treatments significantly relieve residual stresses induced by SLM and slightly reduce the build-orientation mechanical anisotropy observed in as-fabricated parts. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Solution treatment, nitriding and combined effects
- Prior solution treatment and ageing change the response to subsequent thermochemical treatments: nitriding without prior ageing produced cracks in the nitride compound layer, whereas nitriding after prior heat treatment improved wear and corrosion resistance. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}}

Intercritical treatment and austenite reversion
- Intercritical heat treatment around the α′+γ range (example: 720°C) produces reversed austenite that precipitates mainly along martensite lath boundaries, driven by local Ni enrichment from dissolved intermetallics, and this reversed austenite increases pitting resistance in chloride solution tests. {{cite:S4#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}}

AM-specific in situ effects
- The SLM process itself can act as an intrinsic heat treatment causing in situ tempering and precipitation and producing hierarchical subgrain structures and fine precipitates that differentiate AM microstructures from conventional counterparts. {{cite:S6#c1}}

### What these sources do not cover
- Quantitative kinetics (time–temperature–precipitate size curves) and detailed phase-fraction evolution during specific heat-treatment schedules. {{cite:S3#c1}}

