Evidence: A multicenter pragmatic RCT of the BlueStar app found no difference in HbA1c at 3 months (mean difference ITG−WLC −0.42, 95% CI −1.05 to 0.21; P = .19). {{cite:S6#c1}} An exploratory (within‑arm) analysis from the same trial reported that each additional day of app use was associated with a 0.016‑point lower 3‑month HbA1c (95% CI −0.03 to −0.003). {{cite:S6#c1}} A pilot mHealth study in adolescents with type 1 diabetes did not demonstrate HbA1c improvements and concluded a properly powered, longer study is needed to show HbA1c change. {{cite:S2#c1}} Systematic reviews note behavior change effects but call for larger, higher‑quality trials to determine clinical outcomes like HbA1c. {{cite:S4#c1}} 

