Studies find women entrepreneurs using microfinance show greater economic and personal empowerment but weaker political and social gains unless they exert full control over their businesses. {{cite:S1#c1}}  
Ethnographic evidence shows microfinance can increase indebtedness and undermine social capital, which can negate entrepreneurial benefits. {{cite:S5#c1}}  
Social and cultural norms constrain women’s ability to engage in and benefit from entrepreneurship, so business processes that align with or change those norms are also required. {{cite:S3#c1}}  
Interventions that change information access, networks and time use (for example ICT plus network support) produced far larger increases in business starts and profits than mere credit in a quasi-experiment. {{cite:S7#c1}}  
A framework distinguishing personal, relational and societal empowerment implies different interventions and time horizons are needed; credit alone may affect only some dimensions and only after delays. {{cite:S6#c1}}  
Institutional design (mission, subsidies, commercialization) also shapes which clients are reached and how effectively credit translates into broader outcomes. {{cite:S2#c1}}

rows.bRaw:
Credit alone is insufficient because structural, social, informational, and institutional barriers prevent loans from translating automatically into business growth, durable empowerment, or poverty reduction. {{cite:S6#c1}}

### Social and cultural norms
Local social and cultural norms constrain women's agency and limit how much entrepreneurial activity translates into broader empowerment, so business processes must navigate or change these norms to be effective. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S1#c1}}

### Control over business and multidimensional empowerment
Even when women receive microcredit and start businesses, empowerment gains are uneven across economic, social, political and psychological domains, and full benefits require women to exert control over their ventures. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}}

### Networks, information and complementary inputs
Access to social networks and information/ICT amplifies initiation and success of women’s enterprises, implying that credit plus network- and information-based interventions yield larger impacts than credit alone. {{cite:S7#c1}}

### Risk of indebtedness and institutional limits
Microfinance can increase indebtedness and worsen vulnerabilities when not paired with supportive measures, and the objectives and sustainability model of MFIs (commercial vs. social mission) influence which clients are reached and how risks are managed. {{cite:S5#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}}

### Time, culture and program design matters
Empowerment unfolds on personal, relational and societal dimensions over different time horizons and varies by culture, so short-term credit provision without tailored, time-sensitive program design may miss effects on some dimensions. {{cite:S6#c1}}

### What these sources do not cover
What specific complementary interventions (e.g., training curricula, market linkages, legal reforms) work best, and their quantified impacts in Indian settings, are not detailed in these passages.
